00:18:11,081 --> 00:18:15,182
[SPEAKER_00]: We thought of tenting last night on the old campground, but we didn't.

211
00:18:15,982 --> 00:18:21,243
[SPEAKER_00]: We found a cozy little motel in Elkton called Clay's Motel.

212
00:18:21,263 --> 00:18:22,403
[SPEAKER_00]: This is where we holed up.

213
00:18:22,803 --> 00:18:27,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Got there about 2.30, I guess, and spent the rest of the day sorting things out.

214
00:18:27,364 --> 00:18:32,545
[SPEAKER_00]: We had to repack the back of the car completely because of the weight of these

215
00:18:34,160 --> 00:18:36,941
[SPEAKER_00]: There were some pretty heavy ones.

216
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:40,204
[SPEAKER_00]: And then we got up this morning, repacked this morning actually.

217
00:18:40,304 --> 00:18:45,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It was torrential downpour after we got in the motel.

218
00:18:45,727 --> 00:18:48,109
[SPEAKER_00]: It rained all afternoon and evening.

219
00:18:49,010 --> 00:18:51,411
[SPEAKER_00]: We couldn't repack last evening because of the rain.

220
00:18:53,072 --> 00:19:00,037
[SPEAKER_00]: So this morning we got up bright and early and repacked and we're off to King of Prussia in Philadelphia for the baseball convention.

221
00:19:01,060 --> 00:19:09,163
[SPEAKER_00]: And we got there about 9.30 or so, and were there a couple of hours, had a breakfast, and left there at noon, heading back home.

222
00:19:10,244 --> 00:19:17,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And we decided to stop down at Hagerstown tonight and interview Clyde Barnhart, which we just got through doing.

223
00:19:17,206 --> 00:19:20,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Another tremendous interview.

224
00:19:21,768 --> 00:19:26,430
[SPEAKER_00]: We called from Carlisle to make sure they were here before we came down, and maybe that was a mistake.

225
00:19:27,210 --> 00:19:29,071
[SPEAKER_00]: They were very nice, but it wasn't that much.

226
00:19:29,697 --> 00:19:33,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So now we'll talk about the Marquardt interview, such as it was.

227
00:19:33,940 --> 00:19:39,424
[SPEAKER_00]: We could comment more on the interview than the interview could ever tell us.

228
00:19:39,464 --> 00:19:40,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Take over.

229
00:19:40,925 --> 00:19:41,706
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm going to get a beer.

230
00:19:42,886 --> 00:19:49,091
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in the, I put it in with the ice in the cooler.

231
00:19:49,511 --> 00:19:50,211
[SPEAKER_01]: But we went in.

232
00:19:50,272 --> 00:19:52,653
[SPEAKER_01]: The nurse was absolutely delightful.

233
00:19:54,174 --> 00:19:57,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And she offered us a Coke, and she did everything.

234
00:19:57,056 --> 00:19:59,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And then she went to bring him out in his wheelchair.

235
00:20:00,909 --> 00:20:01,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And he came in.

236
00:20:02,309 --> 00:20:04,110
[SPEAKER_01]: My boy's OK, you know that.

237
00:20:07,170 --> 00:20:12,712
[SPEAKER_01]: He came out in his wheelchair, and oh, he was so gracious and nice and so pleased to see us.

238
00:20:14,252 --> 00:20:16,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And Gene started asking him questions.

239
00:20:17,473 --> 00:20:19,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And he started answering them.

240
00:20:20,093 --> 00:20:27,535
[SPEAKER_01]: And then Gene asked him something about, what was your most thrilling moment?

241
00:20:28,315 --> 00:20:29,855
[SPEAKER_01]: And he sat there for a minute.

242
00:20:30,518 --> 00:20:31,959
[SPEAKER_01]: and said, who do you write for?

243
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:39,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was the end of his graciousness and everything else, as you could tell by listening to the tape.

244
00:20:40,844 --> 00:20:49,089
[SPEAKER_01]: But to get back to his apartment, it's not an apartment the way you usually think of an apartment.

245
00:20:49,590 --> 00:20:52,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It's one of these big, luxury, plush apartments.

246
00:20:55,013 --> 00:20:58,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Apparently on this slate drive, there's a series of these.

247
00:20:59,112 --> 00:21:03,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Sort of like the Gold Coast in Cleveland and that.

248
00:21:03,594 --> 00:21:05,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And there were three bedrooms in it.

249
00:21:06,455 --> 00:21:21,162
[SPEAKER_01]: And, of course, security guards, doorman, and reception at the desk that you had to go through to get to them.

250
00:21:22,282 --> 00:21:28,925
[SPEAKER_01]: And the hallway, or the room, was like going into a motel foyer.

251
00:21:29,870 --> 00:21:34,232
[SPEAKER_01]: with empire-style things and the craziest walls I ever saw.

252
00:21:34,792 --> 00:21:38,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of them were deep purple and light beige and then dark brown.

253
00:21:39,814 --> 00:21:42,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And it sounds worse than it really looks.

254
00:21:42,195 --> 00:21:43,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite fancy.

255
00:21:43,956 --> 00:21:46,818
[SPEAKER_01]: It must have been a modern decorator that did it.

256
00:21:48,298 --> 00:21:53,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And he went down a long corridor to get to their apartment, because we got to stay there on the first floor.

257
00:21:55,341 --> 00:21:59,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And, uh, went in, and he went into their living room.

258
00:21:59,672 --> 00:22:10,598
[SPEAKER_01]: And their living room, dining room area was one of these L-shaped things with windows all along one side looking out into the garden to this apartment complex.

259
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:21,064
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was a door that went out onto a little patio where I guess they could run his wheelchair.

260
00:22:22,765 --> 00:22:27,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And those, I'll show you what kind of apartment it was, all those things, all those windows were wired

261
00:22:28,758 --> 00:22:29,518
[SPEAKER_01]: for burglars.

262
00:22:30,719 --> 00:22:31,659
[SPEAKER_01]: And so was the door.

263
00:22:33,219 --> 00:22:38,101
[SPEAKER_01]: But the living room and dining room were done in blue and white.

264
00:22:38,581 --> 00:22:52,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the first time I think I have ever seen the walls were papered, or whatever you would call it, with like a blue patterned taffeta that was absolutely lush.

265
00:22:54,586 --> 00:22:57,727
[SPEAKER_01]: And they had sterling silver all over the place.

266
00:22:58,642 --> 00:23:03,904
[SPEAKER_01]: and a beautiful dining room set in their dining room area.

267
00:23:04,764 --> 00:23:08,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And their stuffed furniture was all blue and white.

268
00:23:09,146 --> 00:23:13,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And she had a modern card table.

269
00:23:14,108 --> 00:23:16,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not a card table, but shaped like a card table.

270
00:23:17,269 --> 00:23:25,572
[SPEAKER_01]: With big fancy legs on it, and then four plush chairs that moved around for her bridge club, because she plays a lot of bridge.

271
00:23:27,263 --> 00:23:29,865
[SPEAKER_01]: But there was an awful lot of silver and stuff in there.

272
00:23:31,025 --> 00:23:33,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So we didn't see any baseball stuff in there at all.

273
00:23:34,688 --> 00:23:44,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And Gene had found out later that when they sold their house, they auctioned off all his baseball stuff.

274
00:23:44,914 --> 00:23:51,818
[SPEAKER_01]: And a man bought it and sold it to this other chap, Sanders, for $50.

275
00:23:51,858 --> 00:23:53,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So you know how much it went for.

276
00:23:53,519 --> 00:23:55,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And Gene will tell you what it's like.

277
00:23:56,035 --> 00:23:57,436
[SPEAKER_01]: The baseball stuff, some of it.

278
00:23:58,717 --> 00:23:59,037
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

279
00:24:00,078 --> 00:24:01,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I've covered the apartment.

280
00:24:02,560 --> 00:24:03,641
[SPEAKER_01]: I've covered the nurse.

281
00:24:05,542 --> 00:24:07,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But you just, if she had been there, it might have been different.

282
00:24:08,825 --> 00:24:09,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But she wasn't, so.

283
00:24:09,765 --> 00:24:10,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was Mrs. Marquardt?

284
00:24:10,906 --> 00:24:11,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Mrs. Marquardt.

285
00:24:12,547 --> 00:24:18,091
[SPEAKER_01]: But she plays a lot of golf and does a lot of swimming and plays a lot of bridge in that, so.

286
00:24:18,212 --> 00:24:21,954
[SPEAKER_00]: They've been married 24 years, so that would take it back to 1955.

287
00:24:23,315 --> 00:24:25,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And the present Mrs. Marquardt must be.

288
00:24:26,986 --> 00:24:35,971
[SPEAKER_00]: His third wife, the sons he spoke of or she spoke of, may have been hers, he called him his stepson because he didn't have any of his own.

289
00:24:36,251 --> 00:24:37,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That he knows of.

290
00:24:37,792 --> 00:24:38,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

291
00:24:38,292 --> 00:24:39,132
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a dad.

292
00:24:45,115 --> 00:24:54,540
[SPEAKER_00]: The collection of Mark Guardana that was auctioned included gloves, caps,

293
00:24:58,875 --> 00:25:07,762
[SPEAKER_00]: plaques, trophies, guides, and probably much more.

294
00:25:07,782 --> 00:25:15,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the only things which were mentioned in kind of a rough way to catalog everything.

295
00:25:17,729 --> 00:25:27,456
[SPEAKER_00]: One plaque he mentioned in particular was in honor of Marquardt's 19 straight victories in 1912, which is still a record.

296
00:25:30,082 --> 00:25:33,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was a pretty big plaque.

297
00:25:33,203 --> 00:25:39,705
[SPEAKER_00]: It had each of those 19 victories noted on this plaque.

298
00:25:39,785 --> 00:25:43,506
[SPEAKER_00]: One could well imagine what that item alone would bring today in an auction.

299
00:25:44,507 --> 00:25:53,289
[SPEAKER_00]: All of this was done many years ago, the auctioning of the Marquardt Anna, which might explain why it went as cheap as it did then.

300
00:25:54,330 --> 00:25:55,270
[SPEAKER_00]: It certainly wouldn't now.

301
00:25:58,922 --> 00:26:03,083
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure that apartment would cost every bit of $700 a month.

302
00:26:04,723 --> 00:26:05,843
[SPEAKER_01]: They said $400 the other day.

303
00:26:06,144 --> 00:26:07,004
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

304
00:26:07,264 --> 00:26:08,224
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been thinking about it.

305
00:26:08,704 --> 00:26:11,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just too large.

306
00:26:12,465 --> 00:26:14,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And all those security features would be added.

307
00:26:16,726 --> 00:26:18,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everybody came out with dripping.

308
00:26:18,586 --> 00:26:23,427
[SPEAKER_01]: All those cars in the parking lot were Continentals and Cadillacs and things like that.

309
00:26:24,807 --> 00:26:27,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everybody in the parking lot had their own number.

310
00:26:29,067 --> 00:26:31,889
[SPEAKER_01]: own numbered parking space that they could drive into.

311
00:26:32,609 --> 00:26:45,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they wanted their car, like that woman came in while we were there, and the man went and parked her car for her so she wouldn't have to get wet, the doorman or security guard uniform.

312
00:26:46,978 --> 00:26:49,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And another one came down and he brought her car to her.

313
00:26:52,942 --> 00:26:54,363
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a cheap apartment.

314
00:26:59,177 --> 00:27:01,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Rube Marquardt said he was 98 or going to be.

315
00:27:02,678 --> 00:27:03,338
[SPEAKER_00]: He meant 90.

316
00:27:03,358 --> 00:27:05,339
[SPEAKER_00]: He was born in 1889.

317
00:27:05,479 --> 00:27:08,359
[SPEAKER_00]: This would make him 90.

318
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:14,521
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a very handsome man, but he would be hardly recognizable today.

319
00:27:14,541 --> 00:27:21,763
[SPEAKER_00]: The only recognizable part was his hair, which was still fairly full, but of course all gray now.

320
00:27:22,343 --> 00:27:27,724
[SPEAKER_00]: But Marquardt's part, which is so well known in the pictures of him without his cap on,

321
00:27:28,475 --> 00:27:38,844
[SPEAKER_00]: was still present, and a smiling face that's rather drained of any real understanding, it seemed like.

322
00:27:39,644 --> 00:27:44,168
[SPEAKER_00]: He's just plain bitter, you know.

323
00:27:44,328 --> 00:27:48,331
[SPEAKER_00]: What he had experienced from writers, we can well imagine he does get into it.

324
00:27:48,371 --> 00:27:49,833
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how well the tape picked it up.

325
00:27:51,454 --> 00:27:56,398
[SPEAKER_00]: When he asked me who I wrote for, there was a transition in the mood.

326
00:27:58,083 --> 00:28:04,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And he suddenly, he didn't become totally hostile, but he just said he wouldn't give out any more information now.

327
00:28:05,485 --> 00:28:07,286
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd give a little bit, but not much more.

328
00:28:08,646 --> 00:28:10,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that he'd given out that much anyhow.

329
00:28:11,227 --> 00:28:22,990
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said all they want to do is to advertise themselves and make a name for themselves, and he and others like him would get nothing from it.

330
00:28:23,010 --> 00:28:27,431
[SPEAKER_00]: I was so taken aback by the question, I didn't really know how to answer it.

331
00:28:30,248 --> 00:28:30,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So I didn't.

332
00:28:32,210 --> 00:28:37,574
[SPEAKER_00]: I kind of fumbled through there for a while.

333
00:28:37,934 --> 00:28:40,636
[SPEAKER_00]: It didn't take long to figure out what his animus was.

334
00:28:43,138 --> 00:28:43,638
[SPEAKER_00]: It was deep.

335
00:28:44,439 --> 00:28:48,162
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was no point in trying to fight it because he couldn't understand you.

336
00:28:50,363 --> 00:28:52,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

337
00:28:53,586 --> 00:28:57,769
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just unfortunate that I came so late.

338
00:28:59,208 --> 00:29:10,450
[SPEAKER_00]: By that, I mean 10 years late, because I know he was such a good interviewee and always willing to take part in programs or panel discussions with old players.

339
00:29:10,650 --> 00:29:12,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had so much to say.

340
00:29:12,570 --> 00:29:14,951
[SPEAKER_01]: I think Lawrence Ritter is the one that finished him off.

341
00:29:15,971 --> 00:29:16,711
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I think.

342
00:29:17,971 --> 00:29:22,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you mention Ritter's book in the interview?

343
00:29:23,412 --> 00:29:25,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't know if that would have done it.

344
00:29:25,533 --> 00:29:26,793
[SPEAKER_00]: There must have been some others, too.

345
00:29:27,133 --> 00:29:28,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm sure there have been others.

346
00:29:30,828 --> 00:29:39,015
[SPEAKER_01]: I really got a lot of publicity out of that.

